    "https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/interview/": {
      "title": "Interview",
      "description": "Interview – Career Center | Westfield State University Class of 2025, take our Post-Graduate Outcomes Survey to let us know where you landed after WSU! Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Career Center Instagram LinkedIn Pinterest X (formerly Twitter) YouTube Schedule an Appointment Donate Students & Alumni Explore Majors & Careers Internship Search Interview On-Campus Employment Job Trends & Salaries LinkedIn & Networking Resumes & Cover Letters Job Search Job Boards, Networking & Career Support Graduate School Employers Employer Sponsorship Register for a Career Fair Recruiting Options Faculty & Staff Career Champions Prospective Students WSU Career Fairs & Events Meet the Team Interview Career Exploration Resumes & Cover Letters Virtual Case Studies with Top Employers Internship Search Job Search Explore Job Market Hiring Trends Featured Opportunities Interview Prep Graduate School LinkedIn & Networking Explore Career Paths & Current Job Market Explore Hiring Employers & In-Demand Jobs Virtual Case Studies with Top Employers On-Campus Employment The interview is a chance for applicants and employers to mutually evaluate the fit between the applicant’s qualifications and the position being considered. Preparing for the interview is critical for your success.  You should be able to discuss why your experiences, education, and skills make you qualified. Identify the key job responsibilities individually and then outline all of your relevant qualifications.  This will ensure that you have enough talking points during the interview.  You also want to discuss what you know about the company and why you are the best person for the job. Checklist Enter the interview prepared, polished, and confident. Review our interview preparation tips belows for additional guidance. Interview Attire Choosing what to wear to an interview can be as stress-inducing as the actual interview.  It is better to go more conservative than too trendy.  Pick an outfit that you feel confident in, showcases your personality, but is also appropriate for the interview. Elevator Pitch You only have a few minutes to sell yourself to a prospective employer–how do you do it? Hit them with your elevator pitch. Questions to Ask During Your Interview Develop a list of (at least three to five) questions to ask during your interview. Research the company and position in detail and determine anything that you might wish to have clarified. Do NOT ask anything that can be found on their website and do NOT bring up salary or benefits. How would I be trained or introduced to the job? How is employee performance evaluated? What career paths have others generally followed after completing the program? As an intern, what kind of projects will I receive? What characteristics best describe individuals who are successful in this position? Where could a person advance who is successful in this position? Within what time frame? In what ways has this organization been most successful over the past several years? What do you consider to be the organization’s weakness as compared to its competition? How is this position important to the organization? How would you describe the culture of your organization? What is your management style? What are you most proud of within your organization? What have you enjoyed most about working here? What are the department’s short term and long term goals? If I work hard and prove my value to the company, where might I find myself in five years? What professional development opportunities are available for employees at this company? What are the key business reasons driving the need for this position? What were the best things about the last person who held this position (or why was this position created?) Describe the three top challenges that I might face in this job. What has to happen for you to know you’ve hired the best person? What are the key deliverables and outcomes that this position must achieve? What would you expect me to accomplish in the first 60 days if hired for this position? Describe the top three initiatives for your company/department and how this position links to these initiatives. What are a few things that really drive results for the company? What are ways in which I can contribute to the company beyond the job description? What are the key metrics for measuring success in this position? How can I best help you and your team succeed? When should I expect to hear from you? Thank You Note Always write personalized thank-you notes within 24 hours to each person if possible. Email or handwritten are acceptable – it depends on how quickly a decision will be made. Paragraph 1: Shows gratitude and makes specific reference to the job. Paragraph 2: References your overall fit and stresses key points working in your favor. When writing to more than one interviewer, you may want to note a question asked by the interviewer or thank them for a specific kindness that was extended. Paragraph 3: Reiterates interest in the position, restates gratitude, and closes on a positive note. How AI Can Help: Generate sample interview questions based on a position description Provide feedback on responses and suggest improvements Identifying transferrable skills from nonrelated positions Sample Prompts: “Create interview questions based on this job description: [paste job description]” “Here’s my answer to “Tell me about yourself” for a [position title] interview. How can I make it stronger? “ “Explain how to talk about my strengths if I don’t have much job experience.” “Conduct a mock interview for a ___ position. Ask me one question at a time and evaluate my responses.” Want more help? Schedule a mock Interview Contact & Location Phone 413-572-5206 Email careers@westfield.ma.edu Website https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/ Address Westfield State University Parenzo Hall, 130 577 Western Avenue Westfield, MA 01086 Career Service Hours Day of the week Hours of Operation Summer Hours M Monday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm T Tuesday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm W Wednesday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm TH Thursday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm F Friday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm Meet the Team Junior Delgado Meet Junior Giselle Frechette Meet Giselle Dino Innarelli Meet Dino Sharon Welch Meet Sharon Lori Putnam Meet Lori Chelsea Rose-Dunham Meet Chelsea Sarah Kraft Meet Sarah Slide 1 Slide 2 Slide 3 Phone 413-572-5206 Email careers@westfield.ma.edu Website https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/ Address Westfield State University Parenzo Hall, 130 577 Western Avenue Westfield, MA 01086 Footer Column 1 Alumni Connections Events Experiences Footer Column 2 Jobs & Internships Organizations Resources Meet the Team Instagram LinkedIn Pinterest X (formerly Twitter) YouTube Privacy Policy | Terms of Service Copyright © 2026 Westfield State University Presentation Close"
    },
    "https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/on-campus-employment/": {
      "title": "On-Campus Employment",
      "description": "On-Campus Employment – Career Center | Westfield State University Class of 2025, take our Post-Graduate Outcomes Survey to let us know where you landed after WSU! Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Career Center Instagram LinkedIn Pinterest X (formerly Twitter) YouTube Schedule an Appointment Donate Students & Alumni Explore Majors & Careers Internship Search Interview On-Campus Employment Job Trends & Salaries LinkedIn & Networking Resumes & Cover Letters Job Search Job Boards, Networking & Career Support Graduate School Employers Employer Sponsorship Register for a Career Fair Recruiting Options Faculty & Staff Career Champions Prospective Students WSU Career Fairs & Events Meet the Team On-Campus Employment Types of On-Campus Jobs Work-Study Jobs Work-study is awarded based on financial need as determined by the FAFSA. Students work part-time on campus or off campus with approved community service organizations. To determine your eligibility or to complete a Federal Work-Study Waitlist form, connect with Financial Aid . Non-Work-Study Jobs Non-work-study jobs are available to all WSU students. These positions are funded by the University and can be found across various campus departments and administrative offices such as University Police , Athletics , Admissions , Dining Commons , and Dunkin’ Donuts . Steps to Obtain Work-Study Complete your FAFSA. Check your financial aid package to see if work-study is listed. Awarded students receive information packets with instructions for securing a job beginning in late July or early August, and as awards are made throughout the term. Search for and apply for work-study jobs in Handshake. You can pick up your contract in the Financial Aid Office to give to the campus department who will be hiring you. Once a student is in the work-study program, the student must continue to show eligibility each year based on the FAFSA. Students who remain eligible and earn most of their award in a year will be awarded the following year. Students who do not participate and earn most of their funds, or who do not show eligibility based on their FAFSA, will not continue to be awarded work-study. If you don’t initially qualify for work-study… Any returning or incoming student may apply for Federal Work Study by completing the Federal Work-Study Waitlist Form provided by Financial Aid , located on the first floor of the Horace Mann Center.  This form does not guarantee a work-study award, but work-study funds can be awarded on a first-come basis. Interested students should reach out to the Financial Aid Department during the first week of classes as demand for work-study usually exceeds the amount of available funding. Interested students must have submitted the FAFSA for the relevant academic year.​ Funds are reviewed periodically, and students are notified of the outcome via their Westfield State University email address. Where to Find On-Campus Jobs Handshake is the platform WSU uses to connects students and alumni to job opportunities, including on-campus roles. All WSU students are automatically registered in Handshake and can log in using MyWestfield. Check out this tutorial if you need help searching for positions. Additional Paid Opportunities Parker Dewey offers flexible, professional, paid work experiences through Micro-Internships. If you are looking for opportunities to demonstrate your skills, explore career paths, and expand your network, but don’t have time for a traditional internship or job, these project-centered experiences are perfect for you – most can be completed remotely, typically involve 10 to 40 hours of work, and are deadline-driven as opposed to set during specific hours. Contact & Location Phone 413-572-5206 Email careers@westfield.ma.edu Website https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/ Address Westfield State University Parenzo Hall, 130 577 Western Avenue Westfield, MA 01086 Career Service Hours Day of the week Hours of Operation Summer Hours M Monday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm T Tuesday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm W Wednesday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm TH Thursday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm F Friday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm Meet the Team Junior Delgado Meet Junior Giselle Frechette Meet Giselle Dino Innarelli Meet Dino Sharon Welch Meet Sharon Lori Putnam Meet Lori Chelsea Rose-Dunham Meet Chelsea Sarah Kraft Meet Sarah Slide 1 Slide 2 Slide 3 Phone 413-572-5206 Email careers@westfield.ma.edu Website https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/ Address Westfield State University Parenzo Hall, 130 577 Western Avenue Westfield, MA 01086 Footer Column 1 Alumni Connections Events Experiences Footer Column 2 Jobs & Internships Organizations Resources Meet the Team Instagram LinkedIn Pinterest X (formerly Twitter) YouTube Privacy Policy | Terms of Service Copyright © 2026 Westfield State University Presentation Close"
    },
    "https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/linkedin-networking/": {
      "title": "LinkedIn & Networking",
      "description": "LinkedIn & Networking – Career Center | Westfield State University Class of 2025, take our Post-Graduate Outcomes Survey to let us know where you landed after WSU! Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Career Center Instagram LinkedIn Pinterest X (formerly Twitter) YouTube Schedule an Appointment Donate Students & Alumni Explore Majors & Careers Internship Search Interview On-Campus Employment Job Trends & Salaries LinkedIn & Networking Resumes & Cover Letters Job Search Job Boards, Networking & Career Support Graduate School Employers Employer Sponsorship Register for a Career Fair Recruiting Options Faculty & Staff Career Champions Prospective Students WSU Career Fairs & Events Meet the Team LinkedIn & Networking Networking is building and maintaining relationships with people who can share advice, information, and opportunities. It’s not just about asking for a job—it’s about learning, growing, and expanding your circle. How AI Can Help: Suggest strengths based on past experiences Draft outreach messages and assist with networking efforts Help write Introductory emails to alumni or professionals Review your LinkedIn profile and suggest edits based on your desired field/position Sample Prompts: “Help me write a follow-up message after meeting a recruiter at a career fair.” “Review my LinkedIn profile and make it more attractive to recruiters in [industry].” “Rewrite my LinkedIn headline to be more attention-grabbing and professional for [desired role/industry].” “Write a concise LinkedIn message to an alum working in criminal justice.” Why Network? Discover hidden job and internship opportunities Gain career insights and advice Build your confidence and communication skills Grow your professional community Where to Start Start with who you know: Friends, family, professors, classmates, alumni, supervisors, and coworkers. Attend events: Career fairs, employer panels, club meetings, guest lectures, and campus events. Use LinkedIn: Connect with alumni, recruiters, and professionals in your field. Join professional organizations: Many offer student memberships at reduced rates. How to Reach Out Be clear about your goal: Are you looking for insight into a career path, internship leads, or general advice? Craft a polite message: “Hi [Name], I’m a [Your Major] student at Westfield State University interested in [Field]. I would love to learn about your career path and any advice you might have for someone starting out.” Check out additional templates . Be respectful of their time: Ask for a 15-20 minute conversation, and be prepared with questions. What to Say When Networking “Can you tell me about your career path and what led you to your current role?” “What skills do you recommend I develop for this field?” “How did you find your first job/internship?” “What do you enjoy most about your work?” “Do you have any advice for someone interested in this industry?” Networking Tips Be genuine: Show interest and gratitude. Follow up: Send a thank-you email after conversations. Stay organized: Track who you connect with and any advice you receive. Keep in touch: Share updates occasionally to maintain the connection. Remember: Networking is a two-way street . Think about how you can add value, even as a student (sharing articles, connecting people, expressing appreciation). Your network is built over time—start small and keep practicing. Need Additional Help? Schedule an appointment Contact & Location Phone 413-572-5206 Email careers@westfield.ma.edu Website https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/ Address Westfield State University Parenzo Hall, 130 577 Western Avenue Westfield, MA 01086 Career Service Hours Day of the week Hours of Operation Summer Hours M Monday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm T Tuesday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm W Wednesday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm TH Thursday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm F Friday 8:30 am- 4:30 pm 8:00 am- 4:00 pm Meet the Team Junior Delgado Meet Junior Giselle Frechette Meet Giselle Dino Innarelli Meet Dino Sharon Welch Meet Sharon Lori Putnam Meet Lori Chelsea Rose-Dunham Meet Chelsea Sarah Kraft Meet Sarah Slide 1 Slide 2 Slide 3 Phone 413-572-5206 Email careers@westfield.ma.edu Website https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/ Address Westfield State University Parenzo Hall, 130 577 Western Avenue Westfield, MA 01086 Footer Column 1 Alumni Connections Events Experiences Footer Column 2 Jobs & Internships Organizations Resources Meet the Team Instagram LinkedIn Pinterest X (formerly Twitter) YouTube Privacy Policy | Terms of Service Copyright © 2026 Westfield State University Presentation Close"
    },
    "https://careers.westfield.ma.edu/resumes-cover-letters/": {
      "title": "Resumes & Cover Letters",
      "description": "Resumes & Cover Letters – Career Center | Westfield State University Class of 2025, take our Post-Graduate Outcomes Survey to let us know where you landed after WSU! Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Career Center Instagram LinkedIn Pinterest X (formerly Twitter) YouTube Schedule an Appointment Donate Students & Alumni Explore Majors & Careers Internship Search Interview On-Campus Employment Job Trends & Salaries LinkedIn & Networking Resumes & Cover Letters Job Search Job Boards, Networking & Career Support Graduate School Employers Employer Sponsorship Register for a Career Fair Recruiting Options Faculty & Staff Career Champions Prospective Students WSU Career Fairs & Events Meet the Team Resumes & Cover Letters Career Exploration Resumes & Cover Letters Virtual Case Studies with Top Employers Internship Search Job Search Explore Job Market Hiring Trends Featured Opportunities Interview Prep Graduate School LinkedIn & Networking Explore Career Paths & Current Job Market Explore Hiring Employers & In-Demand Jobs Virtual Case Studies with Top Employers On-Campus Employment Download a template and start customizing! Focus on showcasing your achievements, skills and experiences so you stand out from other applicants in today’s competitive job market. There are many different ways to showcase your qualifications, but below are some templates to help get you started. You can change the font or overall appearance – just make sure to choose a font and layout that are professional and easy to read. Also, not all sections will be relevant to your experiences.  Delete what you don’t need. Here are a few templates and resources to get you started: General Resume Template Healthcare Resume Template Arts Resume Template STEM Resume Template Education Resume Template Federal Resume Builder Cover Letter Template Cover Letter Sample 1 Cover Letter Sample 2 Laboratory Skills List How AI Can Help: Generate first drafts tailored to specific fields or job postings. Suggest keywords from job descriptions to increase ATS compatibility. Provide feedback on formatting, tone, and clarity. Sample Prompts: “Extract key skills from this job posting and help me add them to my resume.” “Rewrite my resume for a marketing internship.” Get your resume checked by the Career Center. Once you’ve completed your resume, it’s a good idea to have it reviewed by a member of the Career Center staff. We can provide feedback on formatting, content, and other areas that can help you improve your resume and make it stand out to potential employers. Walk-ins are welcome on Mondays between 10 am – 12 pm or 2 pm – 4 pm.  Or schedule an appointment online: Schedule an appointment Skills for Your Resume In partnership with Make sure your resume has the relevant skills for the internship or job you're applying to. There are two ways to use this tool to identify relevant skills to add to your resume: By Keyword: Search for the name of the occupation you’re interested in. By Industry: Not sure what job you’d like? Search by desired industry and see available occupations. Check out the Technical Skills and Core Competencies sections! Find career data by selecting keywords Keyword Search or, by filtering for industry and occupation Industry Search First, choose an industry of interest, then filter for occupation. (If you'd like to see data for a specific location only, filter by state.) Select an industry Agriculture, Food and Natural Resources Architecture and Construction Arts, Audio/Video Technology and Communications Business, Management and Administration Education and Training Finance Government and Public Administration Health Science Hospitality and Tourism Human Services Information Technology Law, Public Safety, Corrections and Security Manufacturing Marketing, Sales and Service Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics Transportation, Distribution and Logistics Select an industry Select